  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts gravity and spacetime at very large scales with familiar emergent behavior in a fluid at much smaller, accessible scales. At the top, a curved sheet with a black hole-like object represents large-scale spacetime geometry, Lorentz symmetry, and emergent dynamics. This is linked to an “unknown” microscopic structure around the Planck length, marked near \\(10^{-35}\\) m, emphasizing that the underlying quantum degrees of freedom of spacetime are not directly known.\n\nBelow, a draining vortex in water serves as an analogue system. The visible whirlpool is the large-scale emergent behavior, while the inset of many blue particles at \\(10^{-10}\\)–\\(10^{-6}\\) m shows the known microscopic constituents of the fluid. The comparison suggests that just as fluid flow emerges from atomic matter, spacetime and relativistic behavior may emerge from deeper microscopic physics.\n\nOn both sides, the figure compares dispersion relations. In the relativistic regime, energy follows the standard form \\(E^2=m^2c^4+p^2c^2\\). At small scales, however, extra momentum-dependent terms appear, giving modified dispersion relations. In the fluid analogue, the same pattern occurs with sound waves and characteristic speeds, showing how Lorentz-like behavior can arise approximately at large scales and break down at short distances.",
